og x scaling across the 10^6 -> 10^7 measurement (extrapolation: ~25x at 10^9). **HL approximation polylog by construction, matches within 0.34 sigma_Pois at 6 cells**: HL_H(x;w) = C(H) * w / log^k(x) at (x_max, k) cells, mean |err/sigma_Pois| = 0.137 across 6 cells. Positive-direction polylog primitive on the k-tuple axis, analogous to Thread 7's R_K(x) and Thread 8's HL_inf(h, x). **What this rules out**: (i) strong H1 hypothesis "Thread-5-shape FAILS to transpose" — refuted, dichotomy transposes (REFINED to H1' bounded-by-sieve-baseline); (ii) hypothesis that speedup is x-independent within polylog regime — refuted, speedup grows with sqrt(x) / log scaling. **What this does NOT close**: (i) worst-case (pointwise) HL error bound — slot 2 target via cross-x ensemble; (ii) decade scaling beyond 10^7 — slot 2 target at 10^8 / 10^9; (iii) named-exponent error bound for HL_H(x; w) — slot 3+ target (cross-x analogue of Thread 8 HLRH); (iv) non-narrow windows w = x^delta. **Cites** E1.5 (cross-x analogue on k-tuple narrow-window axis), S195 (random-phase frame), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template), S418 / S421 (Thread 8 EXACT/APPROX dichotomy + HL evaluator template transposed h-axis -> x-axis), Hardy-Littlewood 1923 k-tuple conjecture (singular-series formula), Tarjan 1985 amortised-analysis decomposition (T_per_x_amort = T_anchor/M + per-query floor — USED final at S224). **Cross-domain ingredient**: no new technique; reuses Thread-5 amortised-analysis decomposition. **Slot 2 recommendation** (highest yield): cross-x HL residual distribution analysis at fixed admissible H, varying x in narrow window — analogue of Thread 8 slot 2's cross-h ensemble at fixed x. Targets: half-Gaussian KS shape, sigma_eff/sigma_pois suppression factor, decade-stability check; building toward Thread-8-shape conditional named-exponent theorem on cross-x k-tuple axis at slot 4/5. See `experiments/analytic/k_tuple_batched/{slot1_baseline.py, slot1_baseline.csv (72 rows), slot1_hl_compare.csv (6 rows), slot1_run.log, slot1_baseline_results.md}`, `archive/sessions/session429_commit_p4_baseline.md`. | 429 |
    895:| §P.P4 asymptotic shape correction of Δ(w) + slot 3 bug fix, slot 4 — extend T(w) computation to K=200,000 (w=1.2×10⁶) to test slot 3's α ≈ 5.72 closed-form fit at the asymptotic limit, and identify the structural form of the leading log-divergent correction. | EXPERIMENT+THEORY (B-REFINEMENT: slot 3's α ≈ 5.72 REFUTED as finite-w artefact; α(log w) grows monotonically from 6.43 (K=50..200) to 9.73 (K=50000..200000); 3-param fit Δ/w ≈ -5.36 log(w) log log(w) + 9.30 log(w) - 22.37 at RMS rel err 1.5%; structural candidate Δ(w) ∼ -12 C₂² · w · log(w) · log log(w) with -12 C₂² = -5.230 matching empirical -5.36 to 2.4%; single-prime Mertens-style heuristic captures 32% of magnitude. Also: slot 3 software bug identified — off-by-one in tail handling biased S_4 high by 1/(1-4/p_tail), 4-6% impact on Δ values; corrected fast eval matches Hardy-Littlewood prime quadruplet constant 4.1511 to bit precision.) | E+I (E1.5 + Hardy-Littlewood 1923 + Mertens 1874 + Gallagher 1976 + Goldston-Yıldırım 2007 + S429..S431 — first multi-decade extension of slot 3's data, first identification of log·log log scaling in HL 4-tuple Cesaro sum) | Thread 9 commit step 4/5 (S432). Built `experiments/analytic/k_tuple_batched/slot4_alpha_derivation.py` (~590 lines): tail-cached fast S_4 evaluator (110k evals/sec, 50-100× faster than slot 3 slow eval) + small_prime_factors_ge5 trial divider + lazy ratio cache for primes > PRIME_BOUND + S4_admissible(m) factored as OTHER_PRODUCT × prime corrections + slow_singular_series (corrected reference) + validation step (max rel err 8e-13) + step 1 (precompute 200k S_4 values) + step 2 (sample H residuals at 62 K's) + step 3 (linear fit on H residual) + step 4 (T(w) for 22 cells) + step 4b (recompute slot-3 cells with corrected S_4) + step 5 (linear fit Δ/w) + step 5b (3-param log² fit) + step 6 (structural candidate test) + step 7 (rolling-band α). **Slot 3 bug**: off-by-one in tail-handling, slot 3 code at line `remaining = [q for q in primes_up_to() if q > p and q <= P_max]` excluded p itself when transitioning to tail-product mode; should be `q >= p`. Bug biases S_4 HIGH by 1/(1-4/p_tail) where p_tail = first prime > diam(H). Verified S_4(0,2,6,8) = 4.1511 (slot 4 fast eval) matches Hardy-Littlewood prime quadruplet constant to 8 digits; slot 3 had 4.4571 (1.0738× too high). Bug impact: slot 3 Δ values were 4-6% LESS negative than truth. **Slot 3 cells corrected** at 6 (anchor, w_lab, w) cells: w=190, K=31: Δ slot3=-3775 → slot4=-3955.6 (+4.8%); w=4071, K=678: Δ slot3=-150,672 → slot4=-159,959.7 (+6.2%). At x=10⁸ wide w=4071: F_pred slot3 0.9137 → slot4 0.9080 vs F_emp 0.9113; |F_diff| stays <0.5% but FLIPS SIGN (overshoot becomes undershoot). **Slot 3 α ≈ 5.72 REFUTED**: extending to K=200k shows rolling-band α(log w) monotonically increases — 6.43 → 6.61 → 7.25 → 8.08 → 9.11 → 9.44 → 9.73 across bands K=[50..200] to [50000..200000], log w_mid 6.4 to 13.3. Linear fit α = 5.07 log log w - 3.31; α/log log w → 3.76 at finite K=200k. **3-parameter fits**: Model A (log² w) coefficient unstable (-0.281 → -0.233 → -0.143 → -0.081 → +0.188 as K_min ↑ from 100 to 50,000) — REJECTED. Model B (log w log log w): coefficient -5.36 stable across fit ranges, RMS rel err 1.5%. **Structural candidate Δ(w) ∼ -12 C₂² · w · log(w) · log log(w)**: -12 C₂² = -5.2298 vs empirical -5.36, rel diff 2.4%. **Single-prime heuristic**: D_p = ∑_j δ(j)·H_p(j) = p/(p-2) where δ(j) = j·6⁻¹ mod p in {1,...,p}; for primes p ≤ K with full blocks q = ⌊K/p⌋, A_p(w) ≈ -6 D_p · q ≈ -w/(p-2); ∑_{5≤p≤K} 1/(p-2) ≈ log log K (Mertens); single-prime contribution to Δ ≈ -24 C₂² · w · log log K = -10.46 · 1.2M · 2.51 = -3.16e7 at K=200k vs empirical -1.08e8. Captures only 32%. **Cross-prime + boundary primes account for 68%** (analytic derivation incomplete; slot 5 GY 2007 target). **Hypothesis status**: H11 (slot 3 bug) CONFIRMED via HL prime quadruplet constant; H12 (slot 3 α=5.72 closed-form) REFUTED by extension; H13 (Δ ~ -12 C₂² w log w log log w) CONFIRMED to 2.4% via 3-param fit, slow asymptotic convergence; H14 (single-prime captures full magnitude) REFUTED, only 32%. **What this closes (slot 4)**: the slot 3 narrative "α ≈ 5.72 closed-form, slot 4 will rigorise α=24C₂²/log(6)=5.838" is closed AS REFUTED — the linear-in-log-w model is incompatible with the asymptotic. **What this does NOT close**: (i) rigorous derivation of -12 C₂² coefficient (slot 5 GY target); (ii) the remaining 68% from cross-prime/boundary contributions; (iii) Goldston-Montgomery zero residual (slot 5 alternative); (iv) cross-H universality. **Cites** E1.5 (cross-x analogue), Hardy-Littlewood 1923 (k-tuple), Hardy-Littlewood prime quadruplet constant (Brent 1975, Forbes 1998 = 4.15118 — slot 4 bug detection), Mertens 1874 (single-prime heuristic), Gallagher 1976 (slot 3 Theorem 1, refined to log·log log rate of approach), Goldston-Yıldırım 2007 (slot 5 derivation candidate), Goldston-Montgomery 1987 (slot 5 alternative), S429/S430/S431 (Thread 9 prior slots). **Cross-domain ingredient**: Selberg-Delange partial-sum machinery for multiplicative arithmetic functions — added to CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md as PROPOSED for slot 5 use. **Closure mode**: REFINEMENT (B-grade ambitious-failure: slot 3's narrative refuted; new structural candidate identified; single-prime mechanism explains 32% of magnitude with cross-prime accounting for the rest, derivation incomplete). **Next-action (slot 5 FINAL)**: rigorous GY 2007 derivation of -12 C₂² log·log log coefficient OR Goldston-Montgomery zero residual analysis OR Thread 9 wrap synthesizing the 5-slot arc as partial-positive Correlation-Dichotomy-shape result. See `experiments/analytic/k_tuple_batched/{slot4_alpha_derivation.py, slot4_h_residual.csv (62 rows), slot4_t_delta.csv (22 cells), slot4_alpha_fits.csv (7 bands), slot4_slot3_comparison.csv (6 cells), slot4_run.log, slot4_alpha_derivation_results.md}`, `archive/sessions/session432_commit_p4_alpha_derivation.md`. | 432 |
    897:| §P.P4 pair-correlation derivation of F_HL_kt under HL, slot 3 — derive closed-form prediction for slot 2 empirical F_HL_kt = 0.87 ± 0.03 via Hardy-Littlewood 4-tuple singular-series sum. Verify prime-by-prime cancellation identity ⟨S_4 factor at p⟩_uniform_m = S_2² factor at p (Bombieri-Davenport-shape mean-value identity); compute T(w) and Δ(w) = T(w) - 2C_2²w² for slot 2 cells; predict F²(x;w) and compare to slot 2 empirical. | THEORETICAL/COMPUTATIONAL (B-PARTIAL-POSITIVE: cancellation identity verified to floating-point precision for first 168 primes; F²_pred matches slot 2 F²_emp to **0.2% at x=10⁸ wide regime**, 1.3% at 10⁷ wide, 3.4% at 10⁶ wide; narrow-regime 4-7% deviation due to discrete-count quantization at mean ≲ 1.3) | E (E1.5 + Hardy-Littlewood 1923 + Gallagher 1976 + Goldston-Montgomery 1987 + Bombieri-Davenport + S429/S430 — first conditional quantitative prediction of cross-x k-tuple variance factor in the project) | Thread 9 commit step 3/(≥3) (S431). Built `experiments/analytic/k_tuple_batched/slot3_pair_correlation.py`: singular_series (with tail-truncation optimization for primes > diam(H)) + factor_S4_at_p / factor_S2_squared_at_p + verify_cancellation_identity + T_for_w + predict_F_squared + load_slot2_summary + main driver. **Cancellation identity** ⟨S_4 factor at p⟩_uniform_m / S_2² factor at p = 1 EXACTLY for all p ≥ 5 (verified max deviation 4×10⁻¹⁶ for 168 primes p ∈ [5, 997]); ratios 2 (p=2 with m even) and 3 (p=3 with m≡0 mod 3) from admissibility constraint. Combined: ⟨S_4⟩_admissible = 6·S_2² = 24·C_2² ≈ 10.4596. **Theorem 1 (windowed-twin Gallagher-Poisson):** under HL 4-tuple, Var[N_2(x;w)]/E[N_2] → 1 as x → ∞ with w/x → 0. Empirically confirmed: F_emp 0.822 (10⁶) → 0.877 (10⁷) → 0.911 (10⁸) wide regime, monotone toward 1. **Sub-leading Δ(w):** T(w) = ∑_{m∈A,m≤w}(w-m)·S_4(0,2,m,m+2) computed for 6 slot-2 cells; Δ = T(w) - 2C_2²w² ranges from -3775 (w=190) to -150671 (w=4071). Empirical fit Δ(w) ≈ -5.7165·w·log(w) + 10.4958·w; intercept matches 24C_2² = 10.4596 to **0.35%**. **F_pred match:** at x=10⁸ wide w=4071, F_pred = 0.9137 vs F_emp = 0.9113 (diff 0.24%); at 10⁷ wide w=3117 0.8907 vs 0.8774 (diff 1.51%); at 10⁶ wide w=2290 0.8552 vs 0.8215 (diff 4.10%). Narrow-regime cells (mean count ≲ 1.3) deviate 4-7% (discrete-count quantization). **Residual F_pred − F_emp > 0** in all 6 cells (0.002 to 0.07), indicating Goldston-Montgomery zeros contribution beyond HL singular series. **What this closes (slot 3):** the slot 2 empirical F_HL_kt = 0.87 question is now structurally explained — leading-order Poisson cancellation (Theorem 1 / Gallagher 1976) plus sub-leading Δ(w) computed from singular-series sum, matching empirical to <0.5% in the cleanest cell. **What this does NOT close:** (i) closed-form value of α ≈ 5.72 in the log(w) coefficient (slot 4 target — structural candidate α = 24C_2²/log(6) = 5.838); (ii) GM zeros residual contribution (~0.002-0.07 systematic positive residual; slot 5 target); (iii) cross-H universality (vary H over (0, 2g), g ∈ [2, 15]; slot 4-5 target); (iv) narrow-regime higher-moment correction. **Cites** E1.5 (Poisson baseline), Hardy-Littlewood 1923 (asymptotic E[Z_iZ_{i+m}] = S_4/log⁴x), Gallagher 1976 (windowed Poisson), Goldston-Montgomery 1987 (pair-correlation for short-interval variance — applied here to HL singular series; CROSS-DOMAIN move from PROPOSED to USED with mode I), Bombieri-Davenport (mean-value identity shape), S429 (slot 1) / S430 (slot 2) baselines. **Cross-domain ingredient:** pair-correlation analysis applied to HL 4-tuple singular series — NEW USE in CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md (mode I, will promote to E if slot 4 closes). **Closure mode:** NOT a closure — slot 3 is positive-direction theoretical extension validating slot 2's empirical constant. **Next-action (slot 4):** derive closed form for α ≈ 5.72 from second moment of S_4(0,2,m,m+2) across admissible m. If derived rigorously, slots 3+4 produce a fully closed-form F²(x;w) theorem under HL → A-grade. See `experiments/analytic/k_tuple_batched/{slot3_pair_correlation.py, slot3_identity.csv (168 primes), slot3_predictions.csv (6 cells), slot3_s4_profile.csv (678 admissible m), slot3_run.log, slot3_pair_correlation_results.md}`, `archive/sessions/session431_commit_p4_pair_correlation.md`. | 431 |
    899:| §P.P4 cross-x HL residual distribution at fixed admissible H, slot 2 — measure σ_eff/σ_pois suppression factor and half-Gaussian KS shape over N=200 disjoint windows per (anchor, H, w-regime), 18 cells across x ∈ {10⁶, 10⁷, 10⁸} × H ∈ {(0,2), (0,2,6), (0,2,6,8)} × w ∈ {log²x, 12·log²x}; methodological question: is cross-x at fixed H iid-like (where Thread 8 cross-x at fixed h FAILED for cumulative π_h)? | EMPIRICAL (B-PARTIAL-POSITIVE: cross-x at fixed H IS iid-like for windowed counts; F_HL_kt = 0.87 ± 0.03 for k=2 decade-stable across 3 decades, MORE stable than Thread 8 F_HL ∈ [0.36, 0.70] by ~5× in range; for k≥3 F → 1.0 Poisson-exact) | E (E1.5 + S195 variance + S418..S421 cross-h template + S429 slot-1 baseline + S240..S244 Thread 7 F_GUE comparison) | Thread 9 commit step 2/(≥3) (S430). Built `experiments/analytic/k_tuple_batched/slot2_cross_x.py`: stratified-disjoint-window sampler (spacing ≥ w + h_max for iid-like residuals) + numpy AND-array pair builder + per-cell KS test against half-Gaussian + decade-stability aggregator. **Headline σ_eff/σ_pois at k=2 wide regime**: 0.822, 0.877, 0.911 across 10⁶/10⁷/10⁸ — F_HL_kt = 0.870 ± 0.030 (3-decade range 0.090). **Half-Gaussian KS at k=2 wide passes**: KSp_eff = 0.624, 0.967, 0.064 at three anchors (2/3 at p > 0.1, 3/3 at p > 0.05). **For k=3 wide regime**: F = 1.020, 1.017, 1.042 (Poisson-exact at variance level, range 0.025). **Methodological correction to S419**: the "within-window cross-x is misleading" finding holds only for *cumulative* counts (π_h(x)); for *windowed* counts (π_H(x; w)) with disjoint x_i, cross-x is the natural iid-like ensemble — slot 2 establishes this distinction. **F-factor comparison**: Thread 7 (cross-x π zero-truncation) F_GUE = 0.755 ± 0.06 (range 0.06); Thread 8 (cross-h π_h at fixed x) F_HL ∈ [0.36, 0.70] (range 0.34); **Thread 9 (cross-x π_H at fixed H) F_HL_kt = 0.87 ± 0.03 (range 0.09)** — Thread 9 axis is more decade-stable than Thread 8. **What this rules out**: (i) hypothesis that windowed and cumulative counts share the same shape-test ensemble (refuted: windowed admits cross-x, cumulative does not); (ii) hypothesis F_HL_kt is k-independent (refuted: F(k=2) = 0.87 < F(k=3) ≈ 1.0 — Poissonian higher-order kicks in at k ≥ 3). **What this does NOT close**: (i) closed-form prediction for F_HL_kt = 0.87 (slot 3 target via Goldston-Montgomery 1987 pair correlation); (ii) cross-H stability across different admissible H at fixed k=2; (iii) pointwise tail bound max|r|/sqrt(HL); (iv) named-exponent error formula for HL_H(x; w). **Closure mode**: NOT a closure — slot 2 is positive-direction empirical extension on a NEW axis. **Next-action (slot 3)**: derive closed-form F_HL_kt prediction via Goldston-Montgomery pair correlation; compare to empirical 0.87 ± 0.03; if matches, slot 3 is A-grade-shaped (new conditional theorem). See `experiments/analytic/k_tuple_batched/{slot2_cross_x.py, slot2_data.csv (3600 rows), slot2_summary.csv (18 cells), slot2_pooled.csv (3 anchors), slot2_run.log, slot2_cross_x_results.md}`, `archive/sessions/session430_commit_p4_cross_x_shape.md`. | 430 |
