    891	| §RE-VERIFY E2.28 — Baker-Norine q-reduced form of D_P^N: was the S161 closure conservative? Specifically, does the divisor `D_Ω₁(n) = 1[Ω(n)=1]` (chips at primes AND prime powers `p^k`, `k ≥ 2`) — NOT tested in S161 — admit a q-reduction identity beyond S161's `{1} ∪ {primes}` support hypothesis? | REFINEMENT (B-grade case (i): two new identities S425.1 and S425.2 extend E2.28's scope; sharpened closure mechanism articulated as graph-topological depth-1 / leaf collection) | E (refines E2.28 inline) | S425 re-verify-closure adversarial probe of E2.28 (S161). **Probe**: `s425_inverse_chipfiring_probe.py` runs q-reduction on six divisors {D_P, D_uniform, D_sink_K=N, D_τ, D_μ², D_Ω₁} × {Γ_N, H_N} × N ∈ {16, 32, 64, 128} (48 runs). Companion `s425_verify_omega1_identity.py` extends to N=256 and prints full chip distribution. **Identity S425.1 (new)**: on H_N with sink q=1, q-reduce(D_Ω₁)·(1) = π(N) exactly. Σ_{v≠1} D'_Ω₁(v) = π(√N) + π(N^{1/3}) + π(N^{1/4}) + ⋯ = #{prime powers p^k : k ≥ 2, p^k ≤ N}. Verified at N ∈ {16, 32, 64, 128, 256} (5/5). Off-sink chips are scattered (NOT confined to prime powers themselves) but their TOTAL is the prime-power-k≥2 count by chip conservation. **Identity S425.2 (new)**: on Γ_N with sink q=1, q-reduce(D_Ω₁)·(1) = π(N) − π(N/2), identical to D_P. Verified at N ∈ {16, 32, 64, 128} (4/4). **Sharpened closure mechanism**: S161 said "building D_P^N requires knowing primes" (input-divisor-level closure). S425 sharpens to extraction-mechanism-level: on H_N, D'(1) reads off the *prime-restricted chip total* via depth-1 collection (q's depth-1 neighbours in H_N are exactly primes ≤ N; chips at depth ≥ 2 are graph-topologically blocked from single-step lending to q and contribute 0 to sink). On Γ_N, sink reads #{leaves of Γ_N with chips} = #{primes p : N/2 < p ≤ N}. **Decomposition lemma**: D_Ω₁ = D_P + D_higher (higher prime powers). q-reduce(D_higher)(1) = 0 because chips at p^k, k ≥ 2, are not adjacent to q in H_N. So q-reduce(D_Ω₁)(1) = q-reduce(D_P)(1) + q-reduce(D_higher)(1) = π(N) + 0 = π(N). **Why closure persists**: identifying H_N's depth-1 neighbours of q is polylog-equivalent to enumerating primes ≤ N; identifying Γ_N's leaves is polylog-equivalent to enumerating primes in (N/2, N]. Graph construction itself costs Ω(N) and includes prime detection. **What this rules out**: (i) D_Ω₁ as an algorithmic-novelty input that bypasses S161's "input requires primes" — BUILDING D_Ω₁ across [1, N] still costs Ω(N polylog) for prime-power testing; (ii) the hypothesis that S161's identity-class is restricted to {1} ∪ {primes} support — refuted by D_Ω₁; (iii) the hypothesis that chips at higher prime powers contribute non-zero to sink under q-reduction on H_N — directly tested, contributes exactly 0. **What this does NOT close**: (i) alternative reduction mechanisms (multi-sink reduction, weighted chip-firing) — outside slot scope; (ii) divisors supported on graph-topologically distinguished sets that DON'T coincide with primes — none currently known. **Verdict**: B-grade case (i) — refinement of E2.28 with precise new statements (S425.1 and S425.2) extending the scope of S161's generalised identity, plus sharpened closure mechanism (depth-1 / leaf collection). Closure stands. **Cites** E2.28 (refined inline), E2.29 (graph-topological closure analogue cross-reference), S161 (parent closure). Cross-domain technique: chip-firing on arithmetic graphs (Baker-Norine 2007 + Dhar 1990) — already USED-I via E2.28; this slot REFINES the scope without importing new technique. See `experiments/algebraic/baker_norine_chi_p/{s425_inverse_chipfiring_probe.py, s425_verify_omega1_identity.py, s425_inverse_chipfiring_results.md, s425_inverse_chipfiring_results.json}`, `archive/sessions/session425_reverify_e2_28.md`. | 425 |
